---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Closing from '../components/Closing.astro';
import { product } from '../data/product';
---

<Base
  title="Why mxwl"
  description="Why mxwl exists: a folder-first editor that brings the operational context around a task together, from browser identities to coding agents and Git review."
>
  <header class="page-hero container">
    <div class="eyebrow">A TOOL FOR THE WAY WE BUILD NOW</div>
    <h1>
      The work changed.
      <br />
      The workspace should too<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="lead">
      mxwl is an editor built around the whole task: the code, the running app, the agent, and the
      decisions you still need to make.
    </p>
  </header>
  <article class="container about-body">
    <p>
      Modern development rarely happens in a single editor window. A ticket might need a worktree,
      an authenticated browser session, a dev server, several terminals, and a coding agent. Then
      you open another ticket. Or move the work to a remote machine. Or restart your computer.
    </p>
    <p>
      The parts are good. <strong>Keeping them together is the work nobody asked for.</strong>
    </p>
    <h2>Start with a folder.</h2>
    <p>
      A repository, worktree, or ticket folder is a useful unit of work. mxwl puts a complete
      environment around it: browser tabs and identities, a code editor, live changes, terminals,
      service logs, and agents.
    </p>
    <p>
      Switch folders, and the context moves with you. Switch hosts, and the workspace still makes
      sense. Your project’s settings remain shared; its runtime sessions stay isolated.
    </p>
    <h2>Keep the human close.</h2>
    <p>
      Coding agents make it possible to move more work at once. They also create more work to
      review. mxwl keeps the agent conversation near the diff, the diff near the running app, and
      the whole fleet near an attention queue.
    </p>
    <p>
      That proximity matters. You can see what happened, decide what comes next, and act on it
      without rebuilding the context in another tool.
    </p>
    <h2>Your tools. Your machines.</h2>
    <p>
      mxwl connects to coding agents through ACP. It works with local folders, SSH hosts, and
      Tailscale machines. Its plugin deck accepts standalone tools made with familiar web
      technology.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is MIT licensed, built in the open, and still an early alpha. You can read the source,
      report the rough edges, and help shape what comes next.
    </p>
    <div class="button-row" style="margin-block:35px 70px">
      <a class="button primary" href={product.repository}>
        Explore the source
      </a>
      <a class="button secondary" href={`${product.repository}/issues`}>
        Help make it better
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
  <Closing />
</Base>
